        <div class="gvm-cloak article-body">
            <p class="lead">
                In the back room of a 112-year-old weekly in
                Silesia, Halina Bednarek cuts a headline out of a
                sheet of laser-printed paper with a pair of
                scissors, trims the left margin with a knife, and
                fixes the strip of paper to a physical page with a
                block of warm wax. The page lies flat on a drafting
                table, part of a sixty-four-page issue that has been
                assembled by hand, in order, every Wednesday for the
                last thirty-one years.
            </p>
            <p>
                Asked whether she has considered layout software,
                Halina — who is sixty-four and has the calm of a
                woman who has never missed a deadline — looks at the
                question the way one might look at a proposal to
                reorganise the kitchen. “The computer,” she says,
                “makes headlines that look like they were typed by a
                computer.”
            </p>
