            <h2>Sixty-four pages by hand</h2>
            <p>
                The workflow has not changed since the 1990s:
                journalists write in a plain text editor, a layout
                team sets the pages in an old program that runs on a
                single, carefully labelled computer, and the pages
                are printed at full size on a laser printer, one
                per sheet. Every headline is then cut out, kerned
                by hand where the spacing is wrong, and pressed onto
                the page with wax — because wax, unlike glue, lets
                you lift a headline and move it six millimetres to
                the left without ruining everything.
            </p>
            <p>
                A full issue is sixty-four pages. The design desk
                predicts, at the start of each week, how many
                cut-and-paste headlines will be needed; the record,
                set during a municipal elections special in 2021, is
                214. The subeditors keep a tally on a whiteboard, in
                pen, next to the kettle.
            </p>

            <h2>The machine in the corner</h2>
            <p>
                In the corner of the room stands the paper’s failed
                pagination system — a desktop computer bought in
                2010, connected to a monitor that still displays the
                setup screen of the layout suite the editors tried
                for six months. Everyone in the building calls it
                “the automation”. It has not been turned on since
                2011. Its power cable is wrapped around its base in
                a neat loop, which is the closest thing the newsroom
                has to a eulogy.
            </p>
            <p>
                The decision to go back to paper-and-wax was not
                romantic; it was economic. The layout suite produced
                pages that required a designer with a diploma, a
                licence fee that grew every year and, crucially, it
                made the pages look like every other page in Poland.
                The hand-made pages took longer, but they cost
                nothing, needed no licence, and — the editors claim —
                the readers could tell the difference. Circulation
                in the district has declined, they admit, but slower
                than the regional average.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="pull-quote">
                A headline is a physical thing. You should be able
                to pick it up, turn it over, and put it back where
                it belongs.
                <cite>Halina Bednarek, chief subeditor</cite>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>What automation saves (and takes)</h2>
            <p>
                The standard argument for moving to modern tools is
                time. The hand-made page takes forty-five minutes;
                the automated page takes four. What the automation
                debate rarely puts on the table is where that saved
                time goes. Here, it mostly goes nowhere: the weekly
                has a fixed number of staff, a fixed number of
                advertisers, and a fixed number of pages to fill.
                The layout software did not make the paper better;
                it made the paper faster at being the same.
            </p>
            <p>
                The editors reached a working compromise that would
                horrify management consultants. Journalists type
                their copy on phones and laptops. The copy is set
                and paginated on the machine. Then it is printed,
                cut, and re-assembled by hand — not to change the
                text, but to change the rhythm: to give a headline
                breathing room, to pull a paragraph back from the
                edge, to make a page feel like it was made for a
                reader rather than for a template.
            </p>
            <p>
                Halina admits the system is absurd. She says it in
                the tone of someone describing a beloved,
                inefficient house. “It is absurd,” she agrees, “and
                it works.”
            </p>

            <h2>The economics of refusal</h2>
            <p>
                The paper’s finances argue, quietly, that the
                absurdity is subsidised by smallness. A weekly with
                a circulation of a few thousand can afford to
                underinvest in technology because its readers are
                not choosing between it and a website; they are
                choosing between it and nothing. The paper’s paywall
                — a small, stubborn implementation that charges
                readers for single archival articles — brings in
                enough to pay for the laser printer and the wax, and
                the editors treat precisely that as the point: the
                revenue exists to keep the craft, not to replace it.
            </p>
            <p>
                It would be easy to present the wax table as a
                monument to nostalgia, and the editors would be the
                first to object. The craft, they argue, is not the
                method; the craft is the judgement. Cutting a
                headline by hand forces you to look at the headline,
                to feel its weight, to notice that “The Council
                Votes” and “The Council Decides” are not
                interchangeable. Automation did not remove that
                judgement. It just got very good at hiding it.
            </p>
