            <h2>Wax and deadlines</h2>
            <p>
                On Wednesday afternoon the issue is closed the way
                it has been closed for decades: the last page is
                printed, cut, and pressed; the subeditors sign their
                initials on the top sheet in pencil; and the pages
                are carried to the back-shop in a stack, where they
                will be scanned, paginated, and sent to the printer
                — a process that, in a final irony, is entirely
                digital.
            </p>
            <p>
                Halina cleans her scissors, locks the wax block in
                the drawer, and writes the next week’s headline
                tally at the top of a fresh whiteboard. Sixty-four
                pages. Somewhere behind her, the automation
                continues to gather dust in its corner, its cable
                wrapped neatly, waiting for a decision that is not
                coming.
            </p>
            <p>
                “Some things,” Halina says, “are made better by
                hand. A headline is one of them. A decision is
                another.” She says this without looking up, which is
                how she says everything.
            </p>
